Task 1: Configure DNS Failover Settings in IdM
Subtask 1.1: Verify Current IdM DNS Configuration
First, let's examine the current DNS configuration on both IdM servers.

Connect to the primary IdM server (idm-server1):
# Check IdM server status
sudo ipactl status

# Verify DNS service is running
sudo systemctl status named-pkcs11

# Check current DNS configuration
sudo ipa dnsconfig-show
Examine DNS zones and records:
# List all DNS zones
sudo ipa dnszone-find

# Check the main domain zone (replace example.com with your domain)
sudo ipa dnsrecord-find example.com

# Verify SRV records for IdM services
sudo ipa dnsrecord-find example.com --name _ldap._tcp
sudo ipa dnsrecord-find example.com --name _kerberos._tcp
Document current DNS forwarders:
# Check current DNS forwarders
sudo ipa dnsconfig-show | grep -i forwarder

# Verify DNS resolution is working
nslookup google.com
dig @localhost example.com
Subtask 1.2: Configure Secondary IdM Server for DNS Failover
Connect to the secondary IdM server (idm-server2):
# Verify IdM replica status
sudo ipactl status

# Check if DNS is enabled on this replica
sudo ipa config-show | grep -i dns
Enable DNS on the secondary server if not already enabled:
# Install DNS on the replica (if needed)
sudo ipa-dns-install --forwarder=8.8.8.8 --forwarder=8.8.4.4 --no-reverse

# Restart IdM services
sudo ipactl restart
Verify DNS replication between servers:
# Check DNS zone replication
sudo ipa dnszone-find

# Verify SRV records are replicated
sudo ipa dnsrecord-find example.com --name _ldap._tcp
Subtask 1.3: Configure DNS Failover Settings
Set up DNS forwarders on both servers:
# On idm-server1, configure primary forwarders
sudo ipa dnsconfig-mod --forwarder=8.8.8.8 --forwarder=8.8.4.4

# Verify forwarder configuration
sudo ipa dnsconfig-show
Configure DNS recursion and caching settings:
# Enable recursion for better failover performance
sudo ipa dnsconfig-mod --allow-recursion=yes

# Configure cache settings for improved performance
sudo ipa dnsconfig-mod --cache-size=100M
Set up DNS load balancing for IdM services:
# Add multiple A records for load balancing
sudo ipa dnsrecord-add example.com idm --a-rec=192.168.1.10
sudo ipa dnsrecord-add example.com idm --a-rec=192.168.1.11

# Verify the records
sudo ipa dnsrecord-show example.com idm
Subtask 1.4: Configure Client DNS Settings for Failover
Connect to client1 and configure DNS failover:
# Backup current resolv.conf
sudo cp /etc/resolv.conf /etc/resolv.conf.backup

# Configure multiple nameservers for failover
sudo tee /etc/resolv.conf << EOF
search example.com
nameserver 192.168.1.10
nameserver 192.168.1.11
nameserver 8.8.8.8
options timeout:2
options attempts:3
EOF
Test DNS resolution from client:
# Test resolution of IdM services
nslookup _ldap._tcp.example.com
nslookup _kerberos._tcp.example.com

# Test resolution with specific nameservers
nslookup example.com 192.168.1.10
nslookup example.com 192.168.1.11
Task 2: Test DNS Failover by Simulating DNS Server Failure
Subtask 2.1: Establish Baseline Connectivity
From client1, test normal DNS operations:
# Test DNS resolution
dig @192.168.1.10 example.com
dig @192.168.1.11 example.com

# Test IdM authentication
kinit admin
klist

# Test LDAP connectivity
ldapsearch -x -H ldap://idm-server1.example.com -b "dc=example,dc=com" "(objectclass=*)" dn
Monitor DNS query performance:
# Create a script to monitor DNS response times
cat > /tmp/dns_monitor.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
while true; do
    echo "$(date): Testing DNS resolution..."
    time nslookup example.com 192.168.1.10
    time nslookup example.com 192.168.1.11
    echo "---"
    sleep 5
done
EOF

chmod +x /tmp/dns_monitor.sh
Subtask 2.2: Simulate Primary DNS Server Failure
On idm-server1, simulate DNS service failure:
# Stop the DNS service on primary server
sudo systemctl stop named-pkcs11

# Verify the service is stopped
sudo systemctl status named-pkcs11

# Check if other IdM services are still running
sudo ipactl status
From client1, test DNS failover:
# Start the monitoring script in background
/tmp/dns_monitor.sh &
MONITOR_PID=$!

# Test DNS resolution (should failover to secondary)
nslookup example.com

# Test specific queries
dig @192.168.1.10 example.com  # Should fail
dig @192.168.1.11 example.com  # Should succeed

# Stop monitoring after 30 seconds
sleep 30
kill $MONITOR_PID
Verify failover behavior:
# Check DNS resolution times
time nslookup _ldap._tcp.example.com
time nslookup _kerberos._tcp.example.com

# Test authentication still works
kinit admin
klist
Subtask 2.3: Simulate Complete Server Failure
On idm-server1, simulate complete server failure:
# Stop all IdM services
sudo ipactl stop

# Verify all services are stopped
sudo ipactl status
From client1, test complete failover:
# Test DNS resolution
nslookup example.com

# Test authentication failover
kdestroy
kinit admin

# Test LDAP operations
ldapsearch -x -H ldap://idm-server2.example.com -b "dc=example,dc=com" "(objectclass=posixAccount)" uid
Monitor failover performance:
# Create comprehensive failover test
cat > /tmp/failover_test.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
echo "Testing DNS failover scenarios..."

echo "1. Testing DNS resolution:"
nslookup example.com

echo "2. Testing SRV record resolution:"
nslookup _ldap._tcp.example.com

echo "3. Testing authentication:"
echo "password" | kinit admin

echo "4. Testing LDAP search:"
ldapsearch -x -H ldap://example.com -b "dc=example,dc=com" "(uid=admin)" dn

echo "Failover test completed."
EOF

chmod +x /tmp/failover_test.sh
/tmp/failover_test.sh
Task 3: Verify Continued Authentication and Resource Access
Subtask 3.1: Test User Authentication During Failover
Create test users for failover testing:
